# keywharf workspace

This directory is a `keywharf` workspace.

- marker: `{{ marker_name }}`
- config: `{{ config_path }}`
- state: `{{ state_path }}`
- host repo path: `{{ host_repo_path }}`
- managed config: `{{ managed_config_path }}`
- managed keys: `{{ managed_keys_dir }}`

`keywharf init` creates workspace files and an empty `repo/` directory at the default host repo path. It does not clone a git repo, initialize git, or touch `~/.ssh`.

If you already have a host repo remote URL:

```bash
# edit {{ config_path }} and set host_repo_remote_url
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} repo sync
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} repo host list
```

If you are starting from scratch:

```bash
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} repo init
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} repo host add <server>
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} repo host endpoint add <server> <endpoint_name> --hostname <host>
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} repo host auth add <server> <auth_name> [--user <user>] [--identity-file <path>]
# if you want a real git repo later, run git init / git remote add / commit / push in {{ host_repo_path }} yourself
```

After your host repo has content:

```bash
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} select <server>
# or pass stable names explicitly:
# keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} select <server> --endpoint <endpoint_name> --auth <auth_name>
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} validate
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} render
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} apply
keywharf --workspace {{ workspace_root }} install-include
```

`select` still accepts explicit `--endpoint` and `--auth` stable names. If one side has a single candidate, it is selected automatically. If one side has multiple candidates, `select` prompts in an interactive terminal and fails fast in noninteractive environments until you pass the stable name explicitly. Local state keeps name-based selectors; singleton selections may leave `endpoint_name` or `authentication_name` as `null`.

Hosts may exist as shells before they are usable. `validate` reports every host that is still missing endpoint options, authentication options, or both. `render` and `apply` only require selected hosts to be complete.
